{"id":2150,"date":"2026-06-02T22:08:02","date_gmt":"2026-06-02T20:08:02","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/fiestas-sant-joan-2026-ciutadellafiestas-sant-joan-2026-ciutadella\/"},"modified":"2026-06-14T15:56:58","modified_gmt":"2026-06-14T13:56:58","slug":"fiestas-sant-joan-2026","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/fiestas-sant-joan-2026\/","title":{"rendered":"Sant Joan 2026 Festivities in Ciutadella: History, Schedule and Everything You Need to Know"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>On <strong>June 23 and 24, 2026<\/strong>, the <strong>Sant Joan Festivities in Ciutadella de Menorca<\/strong> will be celebrated, one of the oldest, most spectacular and moving celebrations in all the Balearic Islands. If you&#8217;re thinking of experiencing this unique event, here&#8217;s everything you need to know: its history, the complete program of events and some practical tips.<\/p>\n<h2>A bit of history: over 700 years of tradition<\/h2>\n<p>The origins of the Sant Joan festivities date back to the <strong>early 14th century<\/strong>. It all began with a religious brotherhood that organized an annual pilgrimage on horseback to the small <strong>rural hermitage of Sant Joan de Missa<\/strong>, located about 3 kilometers from Ciutadella, to honor their patron Saint John the Baptist.<\/p>\n<p>That brotherhood represented the four estates of traditional Menorcan society: the <strong>nobility, the church, the artisans and the payeses<\/strong> (farmers). This structure remains alive today through the <strong>Junta de Caixers<\/strong>, the horsemen who preside over and organize the festivities.<\/p>\n<p>The Junta is made up of the <strong>Caixer Senyor<\/strong> (nobleman, who presides over the festivities), the <strong>Caixer Capell\u00e0<\/strong> (representative of the church), the <strong>Caixer Casat<\/strong> (married man), the <strong>Caixer Fadr\u00ed<\/strong> (single man, flag bearer) and the <strong>Caixers Pagesos<\/strong> (farmers from the north and south of the municipality). Their mission is to ensure that protocols are followed and that all events are celebrated according to tradition.<\/p>\n<h3>The Menorcan horse: absolute protagonist<\/h3>\n<p>You can&#8217;t talk about Sant Joan without talking about the <strong>Menorcan breed horse<\/strong>, declared an official breed in 1988. This noble black-coated animal, related to the Mallorcan horse, is the true star of the festivities. Today it is bred almost exclusively for the island&#8217;s patron saint celebrations, and watching it jump and dance to the rhythm of the <strong>jaleo<\/strong> (the traditional music of the festivities) is an unforgettable experience.<\/p>\n<h2>Sant Joan 2026 Festivities Program<\/h2>\n<h3>June 21 \u2014 The atmosphere begins<\/h3>\n<p>Although the festivities don&#8217;t officially start until the following day, the night of the 21st already has a festive atmosphere. This is when the <strong>lamb is displayed<\/strong> (<em>es be<\/em>) at the home of the Caixer Pag\u00e8s. The garage remains open almost all night and the people of Ciutadella come to visit it, especially the little ones.<\/p>\n<h3>June 22 \u2014 Diumenge des Be (Lamb Sunday)<\/h3>\n<p>The festivities officially begin on this day:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before 8:00 AM<\/strong> \u2014 The <strong>Home des Be<\/strong> sets out, a farmer dressed in a cloak of lambskins (evoking John the Baptist) who carries the lamb on his shoulders. He leaves the house of the Caixer Pag\u00e8s accompanied by the caixers&#8217; procession.<\/li>\n<li><strong>9:00 AM \u2014 First Toc de Fabiol<\/strong> \u2014 The most moving moment: the <em>fabiol<\/em> (wooden flute and drum) sounds for the first time at the <strong>palace of the Caixer Senyor<\/strong>, marking the official start of the festivities.<\/li>\n<li>Throughout the day, the procession tours the <strong>old town<\/strong> visiting the Town Hall, the Episcopal Palace, the Teatro des Born (where the zarzuela <strong>&#8220;Foc i Fum&#8221;<\/strong> is performed), the geriatric hospital and the houses of the knights, inviting them to participate in the festivities.<\/li>\n<li><strong>8:00 PM \u2014 Tirar avellanes<\/strong> \u2014 On Avenida de la Constituci\u00f3n (<em>Sa Contramurada<\/em>) the popular tradition of throwing hazelnuts takes place while the band plays. A very special moment for children.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>June 23 \u2014 Dissabte de Sant Joan (Eve)<\/h3>\n<p>The big day of the eve, with the most crowded events:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>2:00 PM \u2014 First Toc<\/strong> \u2014 The fabioler asks permission at the palace of the Caixer Senyor to begin. The fabiol sounds and the <strong>Replec<\/strong> begins: the procession goes around collecting all the horsemen who will form the <strong>qualcada<\/strong> (cavalcade). The Sant Joan flag is handed over to the Caixer Fadr\u00ed.<\/li>\n<li><strong>6:00 PM \u2014 Caragol des Born<\/strong> \u2014 The most iconic event. More than a hundred horsemen, mounted on their adorned Menorcan horses, <strong>jump and dance in the Plaza des Born<\/strong> to the rhythm of the jaleo. A spectacle of indescribable beauty.<\/li>\n<li><strong>7:30 PM \u2014 Mass at the hermitage of Sant Joan de Missa<\/strong> \u2014 The qualcada heads in pilgrimage to the original hermitage, 3 km from Ciutadella. Meanwhile, the hazelnut battle returns to Sa Contramurada.<\/li>\n<li><strong>9:00 PM \u2014 Caragol de Ses Voltes<\/strong> \u2014 The cavalcade winds through the narrow medieval streets of the center.<\/li>\n<li><strong>10:00 PM \u2014 Ses Corregudes<\/strong> \u2014 Horse races in the Cathedral Square.<\/li>\n<li><strong>11:00 PM \u2014 Caragol de Santa Clara<\/strong> \u2014 Arrival at the convent. Afterwards, farewell to the Caixer Capell\u00e0 and the Caixer Senyor, and the festivities continue until dawn.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>June 24 \u2014 Dia de Sant Joan<\/h3>\n<p>The patron saint&#8217;s day, with the spectacular medieval games:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>8:00 AM \u2014 First Toc<\/strong> \u2014 The day begins.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Morning<\/strong> \u2014 Corregudes in the Pla\u00e7a and Caragol de Santa Clara.<\/li>\n<li><strong>2:30 PM \u2014 Missa de Caixers<\/strong> \u2014 Solemn mass at the Cathedral of Menorca.<\/li>\n<li><strong>5:00 PM \u2014 Replec<\/strong> \u2014 The cavalcade forms again.<\/li>\n<li><strong>7:00 PM \u2014 Jocs des Pla (Medieval tournaments)<\/strong> \u2014 The most spectacular event of the festivities. On the esplanade of the port of Ciutadella, the games of medieval origin are held:<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Romper ses Carotes:<\/strong> Two horsemen gallop facing each other; one carries a wooden shield painted with a caricature (<em>carota<\/em>) and the other must break it with a clay pitcher. The young people rush to collect the pieces as trophies.<\/p>\n<p><strong>C\u00f3rrer s&#8217;Ensortilla:<\/strong> The horsemen gallop with a lance trying to thread a small hanging ring. If they succeed, the band plays and everyone dances.<\/p>\n<p><strong>C\u00f3rrer abra\u00e7ats:<\/strong> Two related horsemen (father and son, brothers, cousins) gallop together and try to embrace and kiss each other on the cheek. A very moving moment.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>11:00 PM \u2014 Corregudes a la Pla\u00e7a<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>12:00 AM \u2014 Caragol de Ses Voltes<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>12:30 AM \u2014 Caragol de Santa Clara<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>5:00 AM \u2014 Darrer Toc de Fabiol<\/strong> \u2014 The last sound of flute and drum at the parish of Sant Antoni Maria Claret. The phrase that closes the festivities is heard: <strong>&#8220;Fins l&#8217;any vinent si D\u00e9u vol!&#8221;<\/strong> (Until next year, God willing!).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>June 25 \u2014 End of festivities<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>11:30 PM \u2014 Fireworks<\/strong> \u2014 A spectacular fireworks display launched from the <strong>Plaza des Born<\/strong>, visible from all over the city, brings the festivities to a close. This year there will also be a free concert with <strong>Cris Juanico<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>Practical tips for experiencing Sant Joan<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arrive early:<\/strong> Ciutadella fills up completely. If you&#8217;re coming from outside, book accommodation well in advance.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dress in black and white:<\/strong> It&#8217;s the traditional attire during the festivities, although it&#8217;s not mandatory.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Be careful during the corregudes:<\/strong> The horse races are exciting but you must maintain a safe distance.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Stay hydrated:<\/strong> The June heat in Menorca is intense and the days are very long.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Park outside the center:<\/strong> The old town is closed to traffic. Use the parking areas set up on the outskirts.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Enjoy the gastronomy:<\/strong> Don&#8217;t miss the pomada (gin with lemonade), the official drink of the festivities.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The Sant Joan festivities are much more than a celebration: they are the essence of Ciutadella, a legacy of more than seven centuries that is experienced with an intensity and emotion difficult to find anywhere else. If you have the opportunity to experience them, don&#8217;t hesitate.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Fins l&#8217;any vinent si D\u00e9u vol!<\/strong><\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>The Sant Joan 2026 festivities in Ciutadella are celebrated on June 23 and 24. Discover the history of over 700 years of this tradition, the complete program of events day by day, the spectacular medieval games and practical tips for experiencing the most emblematic celebration in Menorca. <\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":2,"featured_media":2151,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"footnotes":""},"categories":[72,310],"tags":[313,87,312,311],"class_list":["post-2150"